======================
Guide for Contributors
======================

Our preferred method for accepting contributions is via GitHub pull requests. We
might in the future be able to accept patch files via other means, but at present
GitHub is all we are set up to deal with.

Detailed instructions for setting up a GitHub account can be found at
http://help.github.com/ . A quick summary is below:

1. If you don't already have one, create a GitHub account, then sign in.

2. Decide how you will push your work:

   - Shared repository: if you have write access to the HPCC-Platform repository,
     clone it directly and push topic branches to it.
   - Fork: if you do not have write access, fork the HPCC-Platform repository
     and push topic branches to your fork.

3. Clone the repository (or your fork) to the machine you will be working on,
   using a command like

    git clone https://github.com/<owner>/HPCC-Platform.git

   or

    git clone git@github.com:<owner>/HPCC-Platform.git

   where <owner> is the organization that hosts the repository (or your GitHub
   username if you are using a fork), depending on whether you have set up a
   shared-key login to github or not.

4. Set up commit signing (see "Signing commits" below).

Additional how-to material is available on the project wiki:
https://github.com/hpcc-systems/HPCC-Platform/wiki

Issues
======

Work is tracked using GitHub Issues in the HPCC-Platform repository. Browse the
open issues from the "Issues" tab of the repository.
If you want to work on one that is currently unassigned, add a comment to the issue to
indicate that you intend to do so (to help avoid duplicated effort). If the issue is
already assigned but you think you could help, drop a note to the assignee.

If you think you have found a new issue or have a suggestion for an enhancement, you can
open a new issue on the same page. Bug fixes should be linked to an issue that describes
the problem, with steps to reproduce. For a new feature or a significant change, please
open or reference an issue first so the approach can be discussed.

Topic branches and pull requests
================================

If you want to submit code for a fix or a new feature to the project, the changes should
be pushed to a topic branch (in the shared repository, or in your fork), so that a pull
request referring to your changes can be generated. Accepting contributions via pull
requests allows all contributions to the project to be properly recorded and credited.

It is considered 'best practice' in git for each topic branch (and thus each pull request)
to contain a single fix or new feature. This allows fixes and features to be merged to
the appropriate version without having to cherry-pick which parts of a patch might be
wanted where. You can have as many branches active as you like if you want to be working
on multiple fixes. In general a topic branch should be addressing a single issue in the
issue tracker.

Because a pull-request refers to a branch, not a commit, it is best to avoid using the
'master' branch of your own fork for development work, as it makes it very hard to keep
to the 'one change per branch' rules. Keep your copy of the main branch in sync with the
upstream one, as you will want to use it as the base to check out new topic branches from.

To create a topic branch, use git checkout with the -b argument:

    git checkout -b <GitHub_username>/fix-broken-thing origin/master
    Switched to a new branch '<GitHub_username>/fix-broken-thing'

Branch names:

  - The recommended form is <GitHub_username>/<description>, for example
    <GitHub_username>/fix-broken-thing. Optionally include a type and the issue number, for
    example <GitHub_username>/fix/1234-fix-broken-thing. Only the <GitHub_username>/ prefix is
    checked automatically; the remainder is up to you.
  - Keep names reasonably short but long enough to describe what they are for.
  - Some branch names are used for special purposes in the HPCC-Platform repository
    and must not be used for topic branches - examples are 'master', 'candidate-*',
    'release-*', and 'stable'.

Once the branch is created, you can code and test your changes, and commit them any time
you get to a reasonable point to do so (or if you want to switch to working on a
different branch). You can push changes periodically in order to have a backup or if
you want to discuss the work in progress with other developers. This can be done using
git push:

    git push origin <GitHub_username>/fix-broken-thing

The branch will now show up on GitHub. When you are ready to submit a pull request,
open the branch on GitHub and click "Compare & pull request" (or use "gh pr create").

You can add a comment about the pull request - there's no need to replicate the
information that is in the commits concerning what was changed and why. You can also
use the @mention syntax to ensure that other contributors or maintainers that you think
should see this request are notified.

Linking issues and impacts
--------------------------

Both the pull request description and the first commit message must contain:

  - A standalone line that links the issue, or states that there is none:

        Fixes #1234

    "Closes" and "Resolves" are accepted as well. If there is no issue, use the line

        NoIssue

    The linked issue is closed automatically when the pull request is merged to
    'master' or to a 'candidate-*' branch.

  - One or more "Impacts:" lines naming the affected components, for example

        Impacts: roxie, thor

    The valid component names are listed in .github/impact-components.txt (use
    "Impacts: none" if nothing applies).

Note that additional changes can be made to the branch after you have generated a pull
request - for example in order to address issues raised by a reviewer. There is no need
to close a request and open a new one in such circumstances. Multiple commits are fine
while the pull request is being reviewed; they are squashed when the pull request is
finally merged. Please do not rebase or force-push once review has started unless it is
unavoidable (it makes it harder for reviewers to see what changed since their last review).

If your topic branch is implementing a new feature, it should be based from the master
branch. If it is fixing a bug that cannot wait for the next feature release, it should
be based on the oldest supported candidate branch that is eligible for that type and
severity of fix (see devdoc/VersionSupport.md, which describes which branches accept which
kinds of changes, and devdoc/CodeSubmissions.md). Changes are up-merged into newer
branches.

Keeping your branch up to date
==============================

If a new feature in a branch is in development for a while, you should regularly merge
in the target branch, rather than waiting until you are ready to issue the pull-request
before discovering that other changes clash with yours, or render your change moot. If a
branch has diverged too far from the target branch that it cannot be easily or safely
merged, it is likely to be rejected by the maintainers.

To pull in changes (when working from a fork, first add the main repository as an
"upstream" remote with: git remote add upstream <url of the main repository>):

    git fetch origin
    git merge origin/<target-branch>

where <target-branch> is the branch your pull request targets (master for new features,
or the candidate-<major>.<minor>.x branch for a fix). Use "upstream" instead of "origin"
if you are working from a fork.

Prefer merging over rebasing once a pull request has been opened. Rebase only if it is
unavoidable.

For more information, see http://help.github.com/fork-a-repo/

Commit guidelines
=================

In order for your pull-request to be accepted into the repository, it will need
to fulfill the following requirements:

1.  All commits must be signed - see "Signing commits" below. By signing the commits you
    are signaling that you have the appropriate rights to the change you are submitting,
    and the right to assign those rights to HPCC Systems® under the terms of the
    contributory agreement. We have to be strict about this requirement to keep the
    lawyers happy.
2.  All new code in the commit should follow the coding standards used by our project,
    for layout conventions, variable naming conventions, programming paradigms etc.
    If you are using the eclipse IDE, the file misc/hpcc-eclipse-style.xml can be
    imported into the settings for the C++ code style formatter.
3.  The code should fix a single issue. If you spot other issues in nearby code, you
    should create a separate branch and pull request to fix them (or create an issue and
    let someone else fix them).
4.  The code should pass code review by one of the maintainers (or someone they nominate
    to review it for them).
5.  The code should compile, without warnings, on all supported targets.
6.  If appropriate, new tests must accompany new functionality.
7.  The commit messages must conform to the commit message guidelines described below.
8.  The commits in the branch should progress logically and forwards. Multiple commits
    are fine during review, and are squashed when merged. If you started coding
    something one way, then changed your mind and coded it a different way, consider
    squashing the commits together before the final review so that the reviewer doesn't
    have to waste time trying to understand the blind alleys.
9.  Don't mix code changes with reformatting. This is really a special case of point 3
    above but one that seems to cause particular problems. Correct formatting and
    whitespace issues on the code that you are submitting, but don't go fixing all the
    other formatting in a file just because you changed a line in it - doing so makes it
    harder to review your change or to follow the project history.
10. Submit whitespace/formatting cleanup changes in their own issues, and in small chunks
    (e.g. one file, or a few related files in one directory, at a time).
11. Don't half-fix an issue (unless it's a whitespace cleanup!).
12. Don't introduce new whitespace issues. You can set up your git to automatically check
    that there are no new leading tab or trailing whitespace issues in your commits.

Don't worry if your pull-request is rejected at first - the reasons for the rejection
should always have been explained by the maintainers and it should usually be possible
to address them without starting from scratch. We are fairly strict about most of the
above rules (and extremely strict about some of them) so it's not unusual for a new
contributor to have a few pull-requests rejected the first time they are submitted.

Signing commits
===============

Every commit needs both:

  - A Developer Certificate of Origin (DCO) sign-off: a "Signed-off-by:" line, added by
    git commit -s
  - A cryptographic signature (GPG, SSH or S/MIME) that GitHub shows as "Verified",
    created by git commit -S (or by setting commit.gpgsign to true).

For example:

    git commit -s -S

The pull request checks verify both on the first commit of the pull request.

Commit messages
===============

We follow the Conventional Commits specification (https://www.conventionalcommits.org/).

1.  The first line of the commit (and the title of the pull request) must have the form

        <type>(<optional scope>)<!>: <short description>

    where <type> is one of: build, chore, ci, docs, feat, fix, perf, refactor, revert,
    style, test, deps, release, hotfix, security. For example

        fix(dfu): ensure dfuserver closes files cleanly so errors are reported

    The summary will appear in the condensed form of the changelog generated from the git
    history, so it should make sense by itself. Do not include an issue number in the
    title, and do not end with a full stop. Keep it to about 80 characters.

2.  The second line of the commit message should be blank

3.  The remainder of the commit message should be wrapped at 80 chars, and should contain
    the following information (where appropriate):

      - what the behaviour was before this change.
      - what the behaviour is now.
      - what is the reason for the change.

    Additional information that will be helpful to the reviewer, or to anyone looking at
    the git history, should also be provided. However do NOT include in the comment
    information that is better provided by looking at the diff - any such information is
    at best redundant and at worst contradictory.

    Use an active voice, and describe the effect of applying the change. For example, you
    should say "Add O(n) travelling salesman support" rather than "Adds fast TSP", "Added
    code to make system faster", or "I changed line 32".

4.  Include the issue directive (Fixes #n, Closes #n, Resolves #n, or NoIssue) and the
    "Impacts:" line(s) described above, each on its own line.

5.  The commit must be signed off and signed (see "Signing commits" above).

If you copy the files from the githooks directory into .git/hooks, then git will automatically
check (some of) these requirements whenever you commit. This is strongly encouraged to
ensure that we can accept commits promptly. See devdoc/CodeSubmissions.md for more
suggestions.
